faqs:
  - question: "How do I install Appwrite on Kubernetes?"
    answer: "Install the official Helm chart from `oci://ghcr.io/appwrite/charts/appwrite`. You need Kubernetes 1.29 or later with a default StorageClass, Helm 4 or Helm 3.18 or later, and an ingress controller or Gateway API implementation. Write a values file with `appwrite.domain` and your ingress settings, then run `helm install appwrite oci://ghcr.io/appwrite/charts/appwrite --namespace appwrite --create-namespace -f values.yaml --wait --timeout 15m`. The [Kubernetes docs](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/platforms/kubernetes) walk through a local install on kind and a production install with cert-manager."
  - question: "Which Appwrite version does the Helm chart deploy?"
    answer: "Each chart version deploys one Appwrite version, its `appVersion`, so upgrading the chart upgrades Appwrite. Run `helm show chart oci://ghcr.io/appwrite/charts/appwrite` to see the Appwrite version of the latest chart, and check the [version table](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/platforms/kubernetes#versions) in the docs."
  - question: "Can I use managed PostgreSQL, Redis, and S3 with the Appwrite Helm chart?"
    answer: "Yes. The chart runs built-in PostgreSQL, Redis, ClickHouse, and object storage by default, and each one has a `mode` switch you set to `external` on its own. It works with Amazon RDS and Aurora, Cloud SQL, Azure Database for PostgreSQL, CloudNativePG, ElastiCache, Memorystore, Valkey, ClickHouse Cloud, and any S3-compatible object store. External PostgreSQL needs the `postgis`, `vector`, and `pg_trgm` extensions. See [built-in or external services](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/platforms/kubernetes#external-services)."
  - question: "Why does the Appwrite executor need a privileged pod on Kubernetes?"
    answer: "Builds run as unprivileged Kubernetes Jobs, but executions run on the Open Runtimes executor, which starts a container per runtime through a Docker daemon. The chart gives each executor pod its own Docker daemon, which runs as a privileged container in a namespace of its own. Functions and Sites are on by default, so a plain install runs one. Run it on dedicated nodes. Where privileged pods aren't allowed, set `compute.enabled: false`, or use Sysbox with `compute.executor.privileged: false` and `compute.executor.runtimeClassName: sysbox-runc`."
  - question: "Can I migrate an existing Docker installation of Appwrite to Kubernetes?"
    answer: "Yes. Upgrade the Docker installation to the Appwrite version the chart deploys, put its encryption key and core secrets in a Kubernetes Secret, dump PostgreSQL and the file volumes, install the chart with the API scaled to zero, restore the data, and then start Appwrite with `helm upgrade`. The chart's [migration guide](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart/blob/main/docs/migrating-from-docker.md) has every command."
  - question: "Should I self-host Appwrite with Docker or with the Helm chart?"
    answer: "Use the [Docker installation](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/installation) when one server is enough. It's simpler to run, and Functions and Sites work without a privileged pod. Use the Helm chart when you already operate Kubernetes and want managed datastores, replicas across nodes, rolling upgrades, or GitOps with Argo CD or Flux. If you'd rather not operate Appwrite at all, use [Appwrite Cloud](https://cloud.appwrite.io)."
---

Self-hosted Appwrite has always shipped as a Docker Compose stack. On one server, that's the right shape: one installer, one `docker-compose.yml`, and an upgrade command that rewrites it. Teams that run everything else on [Kubernetes](/blog/post/what-is-kubernetes-an-expert-guide-for-developers) had a harder time. They translated the Compose file into manifests or Helm charts of their own, then kept that translation working through every Appwrite release.

That translation is more work than it looks. Compose starts services in order with `depends_on`, and Kubernetes has no equivalent. Appwrite's scheduler has no leader election, so a second replica runs every scheduled function twice. Database pools size themselves from the CPU count, which inside a container is the node's count, not the pod's. And every upgrade needs a data migration that must run after the new version is up, never before.

Today, we are announcing the **official Appwrite Helm chart**.

It installs self-hosted Appwrite on your own Kubernetes cluster with one command, and upgrades it with `helm upgrade`. The chart is published to `oci://ghcr.io/appwrite/charts/appwrite`, licensed under BSD 3-Clause, and developed in the [appwrite/helm-chart](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart) repository.

# What the Appwrite Helm chart gives you

The chart deploys the same images as the Docker installation, wired together the way Kubernetes expects:

- **Everything in one install.** The API, console, realtime, workers, schedulers, and geo service, plus built-in PostgreSQL, Redis, ClickHouse, and S3-compatible object storage, so a plain install works on an empty cluster.
- **Managed services, one at a time.** Each datastore has its own `mode` switch. Move PostgreSQL to RDS, Cloud SQL, or CloudNativePG while keeping the built-in Redis, and move Redis later.
- **Ingress or Gateway API routes** for the API, console, realtime, function and site previews, and custom domains.
- **Functions and Sites in the cluster, on by default.** Builds run as Kubernetes Jobs, and executions run on the Open Runtimes executor in a namespace of its own.
- **Three worker topologies.** `combined` and `separate` match the Docker installation, and `grouped` is new.
- **Credentials that survive upgrades.** The chart generates the encryption key and datastore passwords once, keeps them across upgrades and uninstalls, and refuses changes that would make your data unreadable.
- **Data migrations on upgrade.** When the Appwrite version changes, `helm upgrade` runs the migration and waits for it to finish.
- **Hardened defaults.** Every pod in the Appwrite namespace passes the `restricted` [Pod Security Standard](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/), and releases are signed with [cosign](https://github.com/sigstore/cosign).

# Install Appwrite on Kubernetes

You need Kubernetes 1.29 or later with a default StorageClass, [Helm](https://helm.sh/docs/intro/install/) 4 or Helm 3.18 or later, and an ingress controller or a [Gateway API](https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/) implementation. You need one of the two: the console and the API share one hostname, and something has to send `/v1` to the API and everything else to the console.

## Try it on your laptop

The fastest way to see it running is a local [kind](https://kind.sigs.k8s.io) cluster with Traefik, over plain HTTP:

```bash
kind create cluster --name appwrite

helm install traefik traefik --repo https://traefik.github.io/charts \
  --namespace traefik --create-namespace \
  --set service.type=ClusterIP --wait

helm install appwrite oci://ghcr.io/appwrite/charts/appwrite \
  --namespace appwrite --create-namespace \
  --set appwrite.domain=appwrite.localhost \
  --set appwrite.certificatesEmail=you@example.com \
  --set appwrite.forceHttps=false \
  --set ingress.enabled=true \
  --wait --timeout 15m

kubectl -n traefik port-forward svc/traefik 80:80
```

Open `http://appwrite.localhost` and sign up. The first account becomes the console administrator, and everyone after it needs an invitation. The first start pulls about 2 GB of images, so expect the whole thing to take around ten minutes.

## Install on a real cluster

On a real cluster, you bring a domain, a DNS record that points at your ingress controller, and [cert-manager](https://cert-manager.io) for certificates. The values file stays short:

```yaml
appwrite:
  domain: appwrite.example.com
  certificatesEmail: ops@example.com

ingress:
  enabled: true
  className: traefik
  annotations:
    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt
  tls:
    enabled: true
```

```bash
helm install appwrite oci://ghcr.io/appwrite/charts/appwrite \
  --namespace appwrite --create-namespace \
  -f values.yaml --wait --timeout 15m
```

The chart checks your values before it creates anything, and when something is missing, the error names the setting. After the install, run `helm test appwrite -n appwrite`, then back up the encryption key the chart generated. Without it, a database backup can't be read.

A few choices are hard to undo once Appwrite holds data: the release name, the domain, the database engine, and the storage device. The [Kubernetes docs](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/platforms/kubernetes#decide-first) list them, with the reason each one is hard to change.

# Bring your own PostgreSQL, Redis, and S3

The built-in datastores run one replica each. That's fine for evaluation and small installs, and it's not where you want production data. Every external service takes the same shape: a `mode`, an `external` block, and a password that comes from a Secret.

```yaml
database:
  mode: external
  external:
    host: appwrite-db.example.internal
  password:
    secretName: appwrite-external

storage:
  device: s3
  s3:
    region: eu-central-1
    bucket: appwrite
    accessKeyId:
      secretName: appwrite-external
    secretAccessKey:
      secretName: appwrite-external
```

Every credential in the chart, including database passwords, S3 keys, and SMTP passwords, accepts `value`, `secretName`, and `secretKey`. Secrets created by External Secrets, Sealed Secrets, or SOPS plug in without changes. The repository also has example values for [AWS](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart/blob/main/examples/aws.yaml), [Google Cloud](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart/blob/main/examples/gcp.yaml), and [Azure](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart/blob/main/examples/azure.yaml), each wiring Appwrite to that provider's managed database, cache, and storage.

Settings without a dedicated value, such as Git deployments or error tracking, go in `appwrite.config` as the same [environment variables](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/configuration/environment-variables) the Docker installation uses.

# What the chart handles for you

Most of the chart's work goes into details that a hand-written set of manifests gets wrong the first time.

**Startup order.** API pods wait until PostgreSQL, Redis, and ClickHouse accept connections. Realtime, workers, and tasks wait until the API answers, because Appwrite's workers exit if they start before the schema exists.

**Singletons stay single.** The scheduler, maintenance, and interval tasks have no leader election, so the chart always runs exactly one of each, and recreates them on updates instead of rolling them. The worker that consumes the `databases` queue gets the same treatment, because schema changes must apply in order.

**CPU-aware sizing.** Appwrite sizes its Swoole workers and database pools from the CPU count. Inside a container, that count is the node's. A small pod on a 64-core node would start hundreds of workers and open far more database connections than you planned for. The chart sets `_APP_CPU_NUM` from each pod's CPU limit.

**Migrations at the right moment.** When an upgrade changes the Appwrite version, a post-upgrade Job runs the data migration once the new pods are ready. Helm reports success only after the migration finishes, and the chart refuses an upgrade that would downgrade Appwrite.

**Credentials that can't drift.** On the first install, the chart generates the encryption key, the datastore passwords, and the executor secrets, and keeps them in a Secret that survives `helm upgrade` and `helm uninstall`. It fails to render if a new encryption key would replace the stored one.

# Functions and Sites on Kubernetes

Functions and Sites are on by default. Builds run as ordinary, unprivileged Kubernetes Jobs in a namespace of their own, started by the Open Runtimes orchestrator. Executions are harder. The [Open Runtimes executor](https://github.com/open-runtimes/executor) starts a container per runtime through a Docker daemon, and Kubernetes has no Docker daemon to offer. The chart gives each executor pod its own, and that daemon runs as a privileged container, which is root on its node.

We kept that trade-off contained. The executor gets its own namespace with its own copy of its credentials, and a NetworkPolicy, on by default, only lets Appwrite in and only lets the executor reach DNS, S3, and public addresses. Give it dedicated nodes. Where your policies forbid privileged pods, set `compute.enabled: false`, or let [Sysbox](https://github.com/nestybox/sysbox) run the daemon without `privileged` (`compute.executor.privileged: false` with its runtime class) on nodes where you can install it, though the chart's CI doesn't test that path yet.

Builds and the executor exchange code through a bucket, which the chart's built-in object storage provides out of the box: [SeaweedFS](https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs), one replica, with its keys generated and its bucket created for you. For production, point the chart at an S3 store with redundancy, and add wildcard certificates for preview URLs. The [Functions and Sites section](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/platforms/kubernetes#functions-and-sites) of the docs has the values.

# Scale workers the way your queues need

Appwrite's background work runs on 15 queues. The chart supports both [worker topologies](/blog/post/announcing-worker-topologies) from the Docker installation, `combined` and `separate`, and adds a third. `grouped` runs one Deployment per group of queues you define, so you can isolate the busy ones without running 15 pods:

```yaml
workers:
  mode: grouped
  groups:
    core:
      queues: [databases, deletes, mails, messaging, notifications, certificates, migrations, webhooks]
    usage:
      queues: [stats-usage, stats-resources]
      replicaCount: 2
    compute:
      queues: [builds, jobs, functions, executions, screenshots]
```

Here the usage queues run on two replicas of their own, builds and executions get their own pod, and neither group competes with emails and webhooks for CPU or memory.

# How we test the Helm chart

Every change is linted with chart-testing and `helm lint --strict` on Helm 3.18 and Helm 4, unit tested with helm-unittest, and validated with kubeconform against several Kubernetes versions. CI then installs the chart on kind, in a namespace that enforces the `restricted` profile. The scenarios cover built-in and external datastores, every worker topology, Ingress and Gateway API routing, NetworkPolicies with restricted egress, and an upgrade from the published chart 1.0.0 that runs the data migration. Every install runs Functions and Sites on the built-in object storage, and a nightly run builds and executes a function inside the cluster.

Each install runs `helm test`, an end-to-end smoke test, and a second upgrade that must keep the credentials and leave every pod alone.

The first release was 1.0.0, for Appwrite 2.3.0. 2.0.0 brings Appwrite 2.5.0, the built-in object storage, and Functions and Sites on by default. The chart follows semantic versioning: only a new major version changes or renames values. Read the version notes before you upgrade.

# When to use the Helm chart

Use the Helm chart when you already operate Kubernetes and want managed datastores, replicas across nodes, rolling upgrades, or GitOps with Argo CD or Flux.

Stay on the [Docker installation](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/installation) when one server is enough. It's simpler to run, and Functions and Sites work there without a privileged pod.

Already on Docker and ready to move? The chart's [migration guide](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart/blob/main/docs/migrating-from-docker.md) moves an existing installation with its data. You dump PostgreSQL and the file volumes, install the chart with the API scaled to zero, restore, and then start Appwrite.

If you'd rather not operate any of this, [Appwrite Cloud](https://cloud.appwrite.io) runs Appwrite for you.

# Install Appwrite on Kubernetes today

- [Kubernetes docs](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/platforms/kubernetes) cover requirements, installation, configuration, Functions and Sites, and upgrades.
- [appwrite/helm-chart](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart) has the chart's source, guides, and example values files.
- [Production checklist](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart/blob/main/docs/production.md) lists what to change before real users depend on the install.
- [Migrating from Docker](https://github.com/appwrite/helm-chart/blob/main/docs/migrating-from-docker.md) moves an existing installation to the chart.
